      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much protein do vegetarians over 50 need?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Most evidence for older adults supports at least 1.2 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight per day, and more if you're strength training — that's roughly 98g for a 180-pound man. The vegetarians in the IMPACT trial averaged just above that minimum. Because many plant proteins are less digestible and lower in leucine, plant-based eaters have extra reason to make sure they reach their target, not just get close."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Does going vegan cause muscle loss?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "It can, if protein and calories drop. In a 2026 trial of adults 65 and older, those who switched to a self-selected vegan diet ate about 20–28 grams less protein per day and lost arm and leg lean mass over 12 weeks, while omnivores held steady. Strength training cut the loss roughly in half in that trial. The diets weren't matched for protein or calories, so the trial can't show that plant protein itself caused the loss."
      }
