  <header class="hero">
    <span class="hero-eyebrow">New Research · Men Over 50</span>
    <h1>Plant vs. Animal Protein After 50:<br><span>What Two New Studies Actually Show</span></h1>
    <p class="hero-sub">Two 2026 trials put plant-based eating to the test in older adults. The results are more useful — and more nuanced — than the headlines.</p>
    <p class="hero-meta">SnapProtein · 8 min read</p>
  </header>

  <div class="article-wrap">

    <div class="quick-answer">
      <div class="quick-answer-label">The Short Answer</div>
      <ul>
        <li>In a 2026 trial, adults 65+ who switched to a self-selected vegan diet ate <strong>about 20–28g less protein a day</strong> and <strong>lost arm and leg lean mass</strong> over 12 weeks. Omnivores held steady.</li>
        <li>In that trial, adding strength training <strong>cut the loss roughly in half</strong> — but didn't erase it.</li>
        <li>In a second 2026 trial, adults 55+ who lifted three times a week <strong>gained lean mass and strength on both</strong> vegetarian and omnivorous diets. The vegetarians ate less protein than the meat-eaters, and their average gains were smaller — but that difference wasn't statistically certain.</li>
        <li>Neither trial could separate <strong>protein source</strong> from <strong>how much protein</strong> and <strong>how many calories</strong> people ate.</li>
        <li>The takeaway: if you're shifting toward plants after 50, <strong>plan your protein on purpose</strong> — and check that you're actually hitting it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="author-note">
      <div class="author-avatar">R</div>
      <div>
        <p><strong>Robert, age 65 — founder of SnapProtein.</strong> A lot of men our age are being told to eat more plants for their heart. That's often good advice. What almost nobody mentions is what a diet change can do to your protein if you don't plan for it. These two studies are the clearest look we've had at that question in people our age.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Why This Question Matters After 50</h2>

    <p>After 50, your muscles respond less to the same amount of protein than they did at 25 — it's called <a href="https://snapprotein.com/blog/anabolic-resistance-after-40/">anabolic resistance</a>. At the same time, more men are cutting back on red meat for cholesterol, blood pressure, or general health. Both trends are real. The question is what happens when they collide: can you eat more plants and still hold on to your muscle?</p>

    <p>Until recently, most of the research on plant vs. animal protein was done in young adults. In 2026, two randomized trials from the Netherlands finally tested it in people over 55.</p>

    <h2>Study 1: What Happened When Adults 65+ Went Vegan</h2>

    <p>Researchers at Wageningen University randomly assigned <strong>72 healthy adults aged 65 and older</strong> to one of three groups for <strong>12 weeks</strong>: a self-selected vegan diet, their usual omnivorous diet, or a self-selected vegan diet plus resistance exercise.</p>

    <p>"Self-selected" means participants chose their own vegan meals. They weren't left on their own — the study included regular nutrition meetings, recipes, guidance on plant swaps, and some provided food. But they didn't get feedback on what they were actually eating while the study ran.</p>

    <div class="science-strip">
      <h3>Study 1 by the Numbers</h3>
      <div class="science-item">
        <div class="sci-num">−20g+</div>
        <div class="sci-text"><strong>Daily protein dropped after going vegan.</strong> Intake fell about 28g a day in week 1 and was still about 19g lower at week 12. The omnivore group didn't change.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="science-item">
        <div class="sci-num">−1.15 kg</div>
        <div class="sci-text"><strong>Arm and leg lean mass (DXA scan) in the vegan-only group</strong> over 12 weeks, compared with +0.05 kg in omnivores. Thigh muscle volume measured by MRI fell 0.61 liters vs. essentially no change.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="science-item">
        <div class="sci-num">~½</div>
        <div class="sci-text"><strong>Strength training cut the loss roughly in half in this trial.</strong> The vegan + exercise group lost 0.62 kg of arm and leg lean mass and 0.35 liters of thigh muscle — less than vegan alone, but still a loss.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="science-item">
        <div class="sci-num">−14%</div>
        <div class="sci-text"><strong>Muscle-building rate during the first 10 days</strong> — 1.09% per day in the vegan-only group vs. 1.27% in omnivores.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>The important caveat:</strong> the vegan and omnivorous diets weren't matched for protein or calories, and the vegan groups also lost body weight. So this trial can't tell us whether the muscle loss came from the <em>type</em> of protein, the <em>lower amount</em> of protein, eating fewer calories overall — or a mix of all three. What it does show clearly: switching diets without a protein plan changed what people ate in a way that mattered.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Study 2: The IMPACT Trial — Vegetarians vs. Meat-Eaters, Both Lifting</h2>

    <p>Researchers at the Amsterdam University of Applied Sciences randomly assigned <strong>108 adults aged 55 and older</strong> (median age 66) to a lacto-ovo vegetarian diet (dairy and eggs allowed, no meat or fish) or an omnivorous diet. <strong>Both groups did the same supervised strength training three times a week for 12 weeks</strong>, and both were coached to eat at least 1.2 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight per day.</p>

    <div class="science-strip">
      <h3>IMPACT by the Numbers</h3>
      <div class="science-item">
        <div class="sci-num">1.24</div>
        <div class="sci-text"><strong>Vegetarians averaged 1.24 g/kg of protein a day; omnivores averaged 1.58.</strong> For a 154-lb person, that's roughly 87g vs. 111g a day. The vegetarians didn't cut their protein compared with before the study — the omnivores simply ate more. Both group averages cleared the 1.2 g/kg target.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="science-item">
        <div class="sci-num">+0.38 kg</div>
        <div class="sci-text"><strong>Arm and leg lean mass gained by vegetarians, vs. +0.66 kg for omnivores.</strong> Both groups gained. The vegetarians' average gain was smaller, but the statistical range of the difference included zero — so the trial couldn't confirm a real gap, or rule one out.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="science-item">
        <div class="sci-num">Both</div>
        <div class="sci-text"><strong>Got stronger.</strong> Leg press strength and chair-stand performance improved in both groups, with no statistically significant difference between them.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>One more detail stood out: among the vegetarians, people who ate more protein tended to gain more lean mass. That's an association within one group — not proof that eating more caused the extra gains — but it fits the overall picture.</p>

    <h2>The Third Study Most Headlines Missed</h2>

    <p>A 2025 crossover trial from the same Wageningen research group fed active older adults (average age 72) <strong>carefully planned vegan and omnivorous diets matched for calories and protein</strong>, 10 days each. Daily muscle protein synthesis was <strong>not significantly different</strong> between the two diets.</p>

    <p>That's short-term data on muscle-building rates, not long-term muscle size — but it points the same direction as research in younger men already eating vegan or omnivorous diets: when protein and calories are matched, the differences between plant and animal diets shrink.</p>

    <h2>What These Studies Show — and What They Don't</h2>

    <table class="practical-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Claim</th>
          <th>What the evidence says</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
