    </table>

    <h2>If You Eat Mostly Plants: How to Protect Your Muscle</h2>

    <p><strong>Eat enough, period.</strong> A diet switch can quietly become a calorie cut. Losing weight on purpose is one thing; losing it by accident while your protein drops is how muscle goes with it.</p>

    <p><strong>Know your number — and actually reach it.</strong> For most men over 50, that's at least 1.2 grams per kilogram of body weight daily, and more if you're lifting. <a href="https://snapprotein.com/blog/protein-per-day-after-40/">Here's how to find yours.</a> Many plant proteins are less digestible and lower in leucine — the amino acid that flips the muscle-building switch — than animal proteins, so landing right at the minimum leaves less margin for error.</p>

    <p><strong>Anchor every meal with protein.</strong> Tofu, tempeh, edamame, lentils, beans, and seitan are the heavy hitters. If you eat dairy and eggs, Greek yogurt, cottage cheese, and eggs make the math much easier. A soy or pea protein shake can close a gap. Mixing sources across the day helps, because plant proteins vary in their amino acid makeup. Aim for a solid serving at each meal — <a href="https://snapprotein.com/blog/how-much-protein-absorb-per-meal/">here's why per-meal amounts matter more after 40</a>.</p>

    <p><strong>Keep lifting.</strong> In both trials, strength training made a real difference. It's not optional.</p>

    <h2>If You Eat Meat: This Still Applies to You</h2>

    <p>You don't have to go vegan to run into this. Swapping a steak dinner for a grain bowl, or eggs for oatmeal, because it's "healthier" can drop 20–30 grams of protein from your day without you noticing. Plant-forward eating can be a great move for your heart. Just make sure your protein comes along for the ride.</p>

    <h2>The Real Lesson: Know Your Number</h2>

    <p>These trials didn't test tracking — that's not what they were designed to do. But here's what struck me: in Study 1, participants' protein dropped by 20-plus grams a day, and the only people who saw that number were the researchers, after the fact.</p>

    <p>If you change how you eat, the simplest safeguard is to see your protein number every day while it's happening. Not every calorie. Not every macro. Just the one number that protects your muscle.</p>

