    <section class="faq-section">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Is plant protein as good as animal protein for older adults?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">It's not settled. In carefully planned short-term studies where vegan and omnivorous diets supplied the same calories and protein, older adults showed no significant difference in muscle protein synthesis. In real-world trials where people chose their own meals, plant-based eaters tended to eat less protein — and that, along with calories, is hard to separate from the protein source itself. The practical answer: plant protein can work, but you need to plan for enough of it.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Can you build muscle on a vegetarian diet after 50?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Yes. In the 2026 IMPACT trial, adults 55 and older on a vegetarian diet who did strength training three times a week gained arm and leg lean mass and got stronger over 12 weeks. Their average lean mass gains were smaller than the meat-eaters', but that difference wasn't statistically certain, and strength improvements were similar.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">How much protein do vegetarians over 50 need?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Most evidence for older adults supports at least 1.2 grams of protein per kilogram of body weight per day, and more if you're strength training — that's roughly 98g for a 180-pound man. The vegetarians in the IMPACT trial averaged just above that minimum. Because many plant proteins are less digestible and lower in leucine, plant-based eaters have extra reason to make sure they reach their target, not just get close.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Does going vegan cause muscle loss?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">It can, if protein and calories drop. In a 2026 trial of adults 65 and older, those who switched to a self-selected vegan diet ate about 20–28 grams less protein per day and lost arm and leg lean mass over 12 weeks, while omnivores held steady. Strength training cut the loss roughly in half in that trial. The diets weren't matched for protein or calories, so the trial can't show that plant protein itself caused the loss.</div>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">What are the best plant protein sources for muscle?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Soy foods (tofu, tempeh, edamame), lentils, beans, seitan, and soy or pea protein powders are among the most protein-dense plant options. If you eat dairy and eggs, Greek yogurt, cottage cheese, and eggs make it much easier to reach your daily number. Plant proteins vary in amino acid makeup, so mixing sources across the day helps.</div>
      </div>

